From: SF2000 Linux <sf2000@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] qpsx: rebuild the event queue after loading counters

LoadState restored psxRegs and immediately rebuilt the event queue.  That
rebuild rebases the emulated cycle counter to zero and adjusts the currently
live root counters.  The saved root counters were loaded later, so their
cycleStart values remained relative to the old, non-zero cycle.  After a
cross-process load this could leave the next VBlank effectively unreachable
and the following retro_run would never return.

Load every device and counter first, then rebuild the queue.  At that point
psxEvqueueInitFromFreeze can rebase both the restored event timestamps and the
restored root counters as one consistent snapshot.

--- a/src/misc.cpp
+++ b/src/misc.cpp
@@ -923,11 +923,5 @@ int LoadState(const char *file) {
 	psxRegs.psxH=psxH;
 	psxRegs.io_cycle_counter=0;
 
-	//senquack - Clear & intialize new event scheduler queue based on
-	// saved contents of psxRegs.interrupt and psxRegs.intCycle[]
-	// NOTE: important to do this before calling any functions like
-	// psxRcntFreeze() that will queue events of their own.
-	psxEvqueueInitFromFreeze();
-
 	if (Config.HLE)
 		psxBiosFreeze(0);
@@ -968,6 +964,12 @@ int LoadState(const char *file) {
 	     || mdecFreeze(f, FREEZE_LOAD)     )
 		goto error;
 
+	/* Rebuild only after rcnts[] has been restored.  This routine rebases
+	 * psxRegs.cycle to zero and adjusts root-counter timestamps; doing it
+	 * before psxRcntFreeze() adjusts stale counters and then overwrites them
+	 * with unrebased values from the state. */
+	psxEvqueueInitFromFreeze();
+
 	//XXX: HACK December 2016 -- see comment above
 skip_missing_data_hack:
